    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "you keep calling it \"temporary costs\" or \"managed adjustments,\" but that sounds like you're dismissing the actual suffering of people living through it right now. if a policy causes mass unemployment or widespread poverty in the short term, isn't it fundamentally unethical to claim it's \"necessary\" just because of long-term projections? how can you justify prioritizing one group's future stability over another group's current survival?",
